          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::428346e86e6d583a8d61c3e7"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0205"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::5e7e721cb1761802b4554575",
          "source_handle": "P0205",
          "field_path": [
            "study_summary_A",
            "problem_or_question"
          ],
          "value": "The effects of gut microbiota and metabolites on ICI responses have been well-studied in EGFR wild-type (EGFR-) NSCLC, but remain unknown in EGFR-mutated (EGFR+) NSCLC. Given that EGFR+ tumors are often immunologically cold and show limited benefit from ICIs compared to EGFR- tumors, there is a need to elucidate whether gut microbiota characteristics can predict or influence ICI efficacy in this distinct molecular subtype.",
          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::428346e86e6d583a8d61c3e7"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0205"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::72b8df7aef468dc5c563524b",
          "source_handle": "P0205",
          "field_path": [
            "study_summary_A",
            "research_scope"
          ],
          "value": "Advanced EGFR-mutated (EGFR+) non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) patients receiving immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) after resistance to EGFR-tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKIs). The study focuses on the association between clinical factors (antibiotic/probiotic use), gut microbiota composition and dynamics, fecal metabolites, and immunotherapy efficacy.",
          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::428346e86e6d583a8d61c3e7"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0205"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::e71a1eb72eede5f1828bbca1",
          "source_handle": "P0205",
          "field_path": [
            "study_summary_A",
            "work_summary"
          ],
          "value": "This prospective cohort study investigated the impact of gut microbiota and metabolites on the efficacy of ICIs in a specific subgroup of NSCLC patients with EGFR mutations. The research enrolled 74 advanced EGFR+ NSCLC patients and collected detailed clinical data regarding antibiotic and probiotic usage. Fecal samples were dynamically collected from 18 patients at four time points: baseline, early on-treatment, response, and progression. Metagenomic next-generation sequencing and ultra-high-performance liquid chromatography-mass spectrometry (UHPLC-MS) were employed to analyze microbial composition and metabolic profiles. The study aimed to identify biomarkers and mechanisms linking the gut environment to ICI outcomes in this population, which is often considered immunologically 'cold'.",
          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::428346e86e6d583a8d61c3e7"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0205"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::ccbb8d260cff2b9ce6c84e79",
          "source_handle": "P0205",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "facet_contributions"
          ],
          "value": {
            "boundaries": "Limited to EGFR+ NSCLC; does not establish causality for probiotic neutrality.",
            "contribution": "Identifies that antibiotic use significantly worsens PFS in EGFR+ NSCLC patients on ICIs, while probiotics have no effect. This contrasts with some studies in other cancers or EGFR- NSCLC where probiotics might show benefit, highlighting the complexity of microbiome interventions.",
            "facet_id": "F1"
          },
          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::428346e86e6d583a8d61c3e7"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0205"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::e1e88637ee445522384bfa4e",
          "source_handle": "P0205",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "facet_contributions"
          ],
          "value": {
            "boundaries": "Based on a small omics cohort (n=18); requires validation.",
            "contribution": "Discovers specific microbial taxa associated with response in EGFR+ NSCLC (Bradyrhizobium guangdongense, etc.) that differ from the canonical markers (e.g., Akkermansia) found in EGFR- NSCLC.",
            "facet_id": "F1"
          },
          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::428346e86e6d583a8d61c3e7"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0205"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::b3702a3197e89eed1f4c5e8f",
          "source_handle": "P0205",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "facet_contributions"
          ],
          "value": {
            "boundaries": "Correlational only; in vivo/in vitro validation is needed as stated by authors.",
            "contribution": "Links specific metabolites (deoxycholic acid, glycerol, quinolinic acid in responders; L-citrulline in non-responders) to microbial taxa and clinical response. Suggests metabolic pathways like tryptophan metabolism and lipolysis regulation as potential mediators.",
            "facet_id": "F2"
          },
          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::428346e86e6d583a8d61c3e7"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0205"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::08db0dba8af6563011c2fd5f",
          "source_handle": "P0205",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "broader_review_uses"
          ],
          "value": {
            "material": "Discussion notes that EGFR+ tumors are immunologically cold and that baseline diversity did not differ between responders/non-responders, unlike in some EGFR- studies."
          },
          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::428346e86e6d583a8d61c3e7"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0205"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::d63c43f8d3cba44a960ee6a2",
          "source_handle": "P0205",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "broader_review_uses"
          ],
          "value": {
            "material": "Identification of 'U-type' and 'Inverted U-type' dynamic trajectories of microbial abundance relative to treatment response and progression."
          },
          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::428346e86e6d583a8d61c3e7"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0205"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::2581070271d137c95ba7e99f",
          "source_handle": "P0205",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "scope_interpretation_cautions"
          ],
          "value": "Findings are specific to EGFR-mutated NSCLC; results cannot be directly extrapolated to EGFR-wild type or other solid tumors without caution.",
          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::428346e86e6d583a8d61c3e7"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0205"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::0aec56d0c003ddcdfad97f7a",
          "source_handle": "P0205",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "scope_interpretation_cautions"
          ],
          "value": "The omics analysis is based on a small subset (18 patients) of the total cohort (74 patients); statistical power for microbial/metabolic associations is limited.",
          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::428346e86e6d583a8d61c3e7"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0205"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::b64e2f1e48a78dcdb499a56f",
          "source_handle": "P0205",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "scope_interpretation_cautions"
          ],
          "value": "The study is observational; associations between antibiotics/microbiota and outcomes do not prove causation.",
          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::428346e86e6d583a8d61c3e7"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0205"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::bf640c1bebb2844ced09d6be",
          "source_handle": "P0205",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "scope_interpretation_cautions"
          ],
          "value": "Probiotic neutrality should not be interpreted as proof of ineffectiveness in all contexts, but rather as an observation in this specific cohort with potentially varying probiotic formulations/durations.",
          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::428346e86e6d583a8d61c3e7"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
